1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:01,960
Picture a capacitor being charged.

2
00:00:02,580 --> 00:00:06,140
Current flows in the wire, but no conduction
charge crosses the vacuum gap.

3
00:00:06,740 --> 00:00:08,320
Now draw a loop around the wire.

4
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:11,140
A flat surface through that loop cuts the
wire.

5
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:14,800
A curved surface with the same edge can pass
through the gap instead.

6
00:00:15,340 --> 00:00:19,000
Can these surfaces give two different values
for the same magnetic circulation?

7
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:23,580
This puzzle takes us from Maxwell's equations
all the way to an optical coating.

8
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:32,880
The magnetic circulation is an integral along
the loop.

9
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:36,220
It has one value once we fix the direction of
travel.

10
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:40,580
Match each surface normal to that direction by
the right hand rule.

11
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:45,700
Ampere's original magnetostatic equation
counts the conduction current through the

12
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:45,980
surface.

13
00:00:46,620 --> 00:00:48,360
The wire surface counts current.

14
00:00:49,080 --> 00:00:50,500
The gap surface does not.

15
00:00:51,180 --> 00:00:54,840
A charging circuit lies outside the
assumptions of magnetostatics.

16
00:01:00,753 --> 00:01:01,773
Pause and choose.

17
00:01:02,493 --> 00:01:07,373
In the ideal vacuum gap, is the electric flux
fixed, does it change so that epsilon

18
00:01:07,373 --> 00:01:11,873
zero times its rate of change equals the lead
current, or do electrons carry that

19
00:01:11,873 --> 00:01:12,893
current through the vacuum?

20
00:01:13,713 --> 00:01:15,493
Sketch the two surfaces before choosing.

21
00:01:16,053 --> 00:01:19,233
Think about what happens to the charge on the
plates during charging.

22
00:01:26,353 --> 00:01:28,653
The changing flux supplies the missing term.

23
00:01:28,653 --> 00:01:33,533
In the parallel plate approximation, the
electric flux across the gap is the plate

24
00:01:33,533 --> 00:01:35,373
charge divided by epsilon zero.

25
00:01:35,373 --> 00:01:37,693
Differentiate with respect to time.

26
00:01:38,133 --> 00:01:42,213
The result, multiplied by epsilon zero, is the
rate at which charge arrives.

27
00:01:42,863 --> 00:01:45,583
Maxwell's correction makes the surface choice
consistent.

28
00:01:46,223 --> 00:01:48,263
Displacement current has units of amperes.

29
00:01:48,803 --> 00:01:52,763
In this gap it describes a changing field
without a flow of charge carriers.

30
00:01:58,787 --> 00:02:01,247
Now drive current up a long solenoid.

31
00:02:01,807 --> 00:02:06,187
Take the magnetic field inside to point along
positive z and grow steadily.

32
00:02:06,947 --> 00:02:09,647
Outside, approximate the magnetic field as
zero.

33
00:02:10,267 --> 00:02:13,767
Place an imaginary circle around the coil,
without adding a conductor.

34
00:02:14,327 --> 00:02:18,367
Faraday's law relates the electric circulation
around that circle to the rate of change

35
00:02:18,367 --> 00:02:20,047
of magnetic flux through its disk.

36
00:02:25,987 --> 00:02:30,667
Cylindrical symmetry makes the induced
electric field azimuthal in this idealization.

37
00:02:31,267 --> 00:02:35,127
The disk flux uses only the part of the disk
where the magnetic field exists.

38
00:02:35,887 --> 00:02:39,447
For a circle inside the coil, that area grows
as radius squared.

39
00:02:40,347 --> 00:02:43,227
Outside the coil, it saturates at the coil's
cross section.

40
00:02:43,697 --> 00:02:45,517
The circumference keeps growing, however.

41
00:02:46,337 --> 00:02:48,457
Divide the rate of flux change by that
circumference.

42
00:02:54,387 --> 00:02:56,847
The positive z magnetic field is increasing.

43
00:02:57,447 --> 00:02:59,427
The magnetic field outside is negligible.

44
00:03:00,147 --> 00:03:04,907
From the positive z side, which way does the
induced electric field circulate outside?

45
00:03:05,627 --> 00:03:08,227
Choose clockwise, counterclockwise, or zero.

46
00:03:08,847 --> 00:03:13,227
Decide whether the local field or the flux
through the entire disk enters Faraday's

47
00:03:13,227 --> 00:03:13,447
law.

48
00:03:20,387 --> 00:03:22,127
The electric field points clockwise.

49
00:03:23,147 --> 00:03:27,167
Positive flux increases, so circulation is
negative for the counterclockwise

50
00:03:27,167 --> 00:03:27,727
orientation.

51
00:03:28,687 --> 00:03:32,687
Beyond the coil radius, its magnitude
decreases inversely with radius.

52
00:03:33,307 --> 00:03:36,467
The field can be nonzero even where its local
curl vanishes.

53
00:03:37,067 --> 00:03:42,067
Outside the coil, a spanning disk for a loop
encircling the axis also includes the

54
00:03:42,067 --> 00:03:42,427
interior.

55
00:03:43,267 --> 00:03:47,387
This distinction between local and integral
statements is worth slowing down for.

56
00:03:53,220 --> 00:03:56,620
Curl measures circulation per area in the
small-loop limit.

57
00:03:57,420 --> 00:04:02,100
To see how local curls build up, imagine
tiling a disk with tiny oriented loops.

58
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:07,180
On shared edges, neighboring loops traverse
the same edge in opposite directions.

59
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:08,800
Those contributions cancel.

60
00:04:09,430 --> 00:04:13,330
Summing all of the little loops leaves
circulation along the outside boundary.

61
00:04:19,220 --> 00:04:23,060
Take a tiny disk in the exterior that does not
enclose the solenoid.

62
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:25,620
Its normal is still along positive z.

63
00:04:26,260 --> 00:04:29,960
The changing magnetic flux through that disk
is zero in this model.

64
00:04:30,420 --> 00:04:34,940
Yet a probe at a point on the disk detects a
nonzero induced electric field.

65
00:04:35,440 --> 00:04:38,960
A curl measurement compares contributions
around the whole small loop.

66
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:41,360
It is different from a point measurement of
the field.

67
00:04:47,253 --> 00:04:50,113
For the small exterior disk, which statement
is required?

68
00:04:50,753 --> 00:04:54,673
Its electric circulation vanishes, the
electric field vanishes everywhere along its

69
00:04:54,673 --> 00:04:58,293
edge, or the circulation equals that of the
larger loop surrounding the coil?

70
00:04:58,793 --> 00:05:03,393
Choose an answer and explain how the result
depends on which region the disk spans.

71
00:05:10,553 --> 00:05:12,673
The small-loop circulation vanishes.

72
00:05:13,373 --> 00:05:15,933
The field contributions cancel in the line
integral.

73
00:05:16,593 --> 00:05:20,893
In differential form, the curl of the electric
field equals minus the time derivative

74
00:05:20,893 --> 00:05:22,133
of the magnetic field.

75
00:05:22,893 --> 00:05:27,053
Integrate this relation over the oriented
surface, then apply Stokes' theorem.

76
00:05:27,533 --> 00:05:32,393
Reversing the chosen normal reverses the loop
direction and both sides change sign

77
00:05:32,393 --> 00:05:32,733
together.

78
00:05:38,687 --> 00:05:41,107
Charge conservation gives a useful diagnostic.

79
00:05:41,967 --> 00:05:43,047
Choose a fixed volume.

80
00:05:43,527 --> 00:05:47,087
Count the charge inside, and count the current
leaving through its closed surface.

81
00:05:47,887 --> 00:05:51,007
If more charge flows out than in, the stored
charge falls.

82
00:05:51,557 --> 00:05:55,737
The divergence theorem converts the outward
flux into a volume integral of the

83
00:05:55,737 --> 00:05:56,177
divergence.

84
00:05:56,977 --> 00:06:01,197
Since the choice of volume is arbitrary, we
obtain the local continuity equation.

85
00:06:07,153 --> 00:06:11,353
In a straight steady conductor that narrows,
the same current passes through every

86
00:06:11,353 --> 00:06:15,533
cross section, while the average current
density increases in the narrow section.

87
00:06:16,193 --> 00:06:18,593
A plot of that axial average can slope upward.

88
00:06:19,113 --> 00:06:20,733
It is not the three-dimensional divergence.

89
00:06:21,463 --> 00:06:25,883
Near a taper the current field can have
transverse components, and an insulating wall

90
00:06:25,883 --> 00:06:27,683
contributes no outward charge flux.

91
00:06:33,653 --> 00:06:37,013
Suppose the lead current is steady and the
conductor's sides are insulated.

92
00:06:37,593 --> 00:06:41,693
Through a volume that spans the constriction,
is the net outward current positive,

93
00:06:42,133 --> 00:06:43,093
negative, or zero?

94
00:06:43,813 --> 00:06:47,073
Draw arrows for the two end faces and label
the outward normals.

95
00:06:47,543 --> 00:06:51,063
Decide whether a higher current density
implies a higher current.

96
00:06:58,087 --> 00:06:59,407
The net flux is zero.

97
00:07:00,027 --> 00:07:03,747
The same current enters and leaves, so charge
does not accumulate.

98
00:07:04,567 --> 00:07:07,387
Now take the divergence of the Ampere-Maxwell
equation.

99
00:07:08,107 --> 00:07:10,967
The divergence of the left hand curl vanishes
identically.

100
00:07:11,407 --> 00:07:15,667
Gauss's law turns the divergence of the
changing electric field term into the time

101
00:07:15,667 --> 00:07:17,227
derivative of charge density.

102
00:07:17,847 --> 00:07:20,267
What remains on the right is the continuity
equation.

103
00:07:26,253 --> 00:07:29,233
The capacitor stores electric field energy as
it charges.

104
00:07:29,993 --> 00:07:31,433
How does that energy reach the gap?

105
00:07:32,113 --> 00:07:34,993
Take the electric field to point up, along
positive z.

106
00:07:35,693 --> 00:07:40,013
In a circular capacitor, the magnetic field in
the gap points around the axis in the

107
00:07:40,013 --> 00:07:41,453
positive azimuthal direction.

108
00:07:42,063 --> 00:07:46,183
The cross product of the electric and magnetic
fields points radially inward.

109
00:07:52,087 --> 00:07:56,047
Poynting's theorem follows by dotting the curl
equations with the appropriate fields

110
00:07:56,047 --> 00:07:58,767
and using the divergence identity for a cross
product.

111
00:07:59,687 --> 00:08:04,387
The result separates the rate of change of
field energy, the outward energy flux and

112
00:08:04,387 --> 00:08:06,527
the rate at which fields do work on matter.

113
00:08:07,077 --> 00:08:09,377
In the vacuum gap, the work term is zero.

114
00:08:09,997 --> 00:08:13,897
For this quasistatic example, the leading
change is the electric energy.

115
00:08:19,820 --> 00:08:24,040
For the gap volume, what is the sign of the
outward electromagnetic power through its

116
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:25,380
curved side during charging?

117
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:28,240
Choose positive, zero, or negative.

118
00:08:28,900 --> 00:08:32,620
Use the outward normal, not the direction in
which you happen to draw the integration

119
00:08:32,620 --> 00:08:33,020
arrow.

120
00:08:33,460 --> 00:08:36,920
Then ask how this sign relates to the growing
electric energy.

121
00:08:43,920 --> 00:08:45,440
The outward power is negative.

122
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:49,400
Integrate the radial Poynting flux over the
cylindrical side.

123
00:08:50,080 --> 00:08:54,820
Using the Ampere-Maxwell field at the rim, the
inward power is voltage times current.

124
00:08:55,500 --> 00:08:59,820
Independently, differentiate one half times
capacitance times voltage squared.

125
00:09:00,260 --> 00:09:01,340
It gives the same power.

126
00:09:01,810 --> 00:09:06,030
In the lab you will check both calculations
and estimate when neglecting changing

127
00:09:06,030 --> 00:09:08,630
magnetic energy and edge fringing is
reasonable.

128
00:09:14,653 --> 00:09:18,133
Move from slowly changing fields to a
traveling plane wave.

129
00:09:18,753 --> 00:09:21,413
Its electric and magnetic fields are
transverse.

130
00:09:22,333 --> 00:09:26,513
The ratio of electric field to magnetic field
intensity is the wave impedance.

131
00:09:27,313 --> 00:09:31,193
At a plane interface, match tangential
electric and magnetic fields.

132
00:09:31,793 --> 00:09:36,153
For the reflected wave the magnetic field
changes sign relative to the electric field,

133
00:09:36,153 --> 00:09:38,813
because the propagation direction reverses.

134
00:09:44,687 --> 00:09:47,427
Take two linear lossless nonmagnetic media.

135
00:09:48,047 --> 00:09:51,427
The impedance is the vacuum impedance divided
by refractive index.

136
00:09:52,167 --> 00:09:56,227
Solve the two matching equations for the
reflected and transmitted electric amplitudes.

137
00:09:56,887 --> 00:10:00,927
To compute average power, square the electric
amplitude and divide by twice the

138
00:10:00,927 --> 00:10:01,227
impedance.

139
00:10:01,887 --> 00:10:06,627
A transmitted amplitude of one half, for
example, does not by itself tell you the

140
00:10:06,627 --> 00:10:07,887
fraction of power transmitted.

141
00:10:13,820 --> 00:10:17,800
Send a wave from index one into index one
point five at normal incidence.

142
00:10:18,420 --> 00:10:22,740
Relative to the incident electric field at the
interface, does the reflected electric

143
00:10:22,740 --> 00:10:26,040
field have a minus sign, a plus sign, or zero
amplitude?

144
00:10:26,780 --> 00:10:27,580
Choose before solving.

145
00:10:28,140 --> 00:10:32,320
Use both matching conditions and keep the
forward and backward magnetic field signs

146
00:10:32,320 --> 00:10:32,780
separate.

147
00:10:39,853 --> 00:10:43,693
The reflected electric amplitude has a minus
sign, giving a phase reversal at the

148
00:10:43,693 --> 00:10:44,053
interface.

149
00:10:44,833 --> 00:10:47,473
The reflected power fraction is four percent.

150
00:10:47,473 --> 00:10:52,113
The transmitted electric amplitude is zero
point eight, while transmitted power is

151
00:10:52,113 --> 00:10:53,373
ninety-six percent.

152
00:10:53,373 --> 00:10:55,613
The impedance factor makes the difference.

153
00:10:56,213 --> 00:11:01,493
Check both field residuals and power
conservation; either check alone can miss a

154
00:11:01,493 --> 00:11:01,793
mistake.

155
00:11:07,787 --> 00:11:10,327
Now put a transparent film between air and
glass.

156
00:11:11,067 --> 00:11:14,767
Reflection can come from both boundaries, and
the field travels through the film in

157
00:11:14,767 --> 00:11:15,527
both directions.

158
00:11:16,147 --> 00:11:19,867
A film a quarter wavelength thick gives a
half-cycle of round-trip phase.

159
00:11:20,417 --> 00:11:25,097
To cancel reflection completely at the design
wavelength, the returning amplitudes must

160
00:11:25,097 --> 00:11:25,897
also balance.

161
00:11:26,717 --> 00:11:27,637
Thickness sets phase.

162
00:11:28,277 --> 00:11:30,017
Impedance sets the amplitude matching.

163
00:11:36,020 --> 00:11:41,100
For nonmagnetic lossless media at normal
incidence, choose the film index as the

164
00:11:41,100 --> 00:11:43,080
geometric mean of the outer indices.

165
00:11:43,860 --> 00:11:48,240
Set thickness to the vacuum design wavelength
divided by four times that film index.

166
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:52,260
This ideal match eliminates reflection at the
design wavelength.

167
00:11:52,830 --> 00:11:58,470
A finite spectral band, material dispersion,
absorption, or oblique incidence changes

168
00:11:58,470 --> 00:11:58,990
the result.

169
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:08,840
Change the illumination wavelength while
holding film thickness and indices fixed.

170
00:12:09,520 --> 00:12:14,560
Does the cancellation generally remain exact,
does reflected power generally return, or

171
00:12:14,560 --> 00:12:16,340
is all incident power now absorbed?

172
00:12:17,200 --> 00:12:19,520
Think about the phase accrued in one
traversal.

173
00:12:19,950 --> 00:12:23,070
State which assumptions exclude energy loss
before choosing.

174
00:12:30,120 --> 00:12:32,040
Reflected power generally returns.

175
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:36,580
The physical film stays the same, but its
optical phase changes with wavelength.

176
00:12:37,400 --> 00:12:40,800
In the Python lab, compare a boundary-matching
solver with a separate

177
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:42,160
multiple-reflection formula.

178
00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:47,260
Then design a coating for a new substrate and
report how thickness tolerance shifts the

179
00:12:47,260 --> 00:12:48,140
reflectance curve.

180
00:12:48,700 --> 00:12:52,620
Explain what a real experiment would need to
measure, and which conclusion would fail

181
00:12:52,620 --> 00:12:54,360
first if your assumptions changed.
